{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# DC renderer robustness & performance hardening\n\n## Context / Problem\nThe DC path shells out to pandoc per field, which introduces three production\nhazards proven in the DC experiment: (1) a per-field subprocess tax — latency is a\nflat ~0.3s/field spawn cost independent of body size, and a serial reconcile pass\nover many fields blocked ~20 min of wall time on `fork+exec`; (2) a\npathological-input hang — one corpus body drove pandoc's jira reader to spin\n13.5 min at 95.8% CPU, and pypandoc invokes pandoc with no timeout, so one field\ncan stall the whole reconcile indefinitely; (3) pandoc is GPL and rides in the\n`pypandoc-binary` wheel, so its provenance and license must be pinned and recorded.\nThese must be designed against before the DC codec ships live.\n\n## Prior art / dependency boundaries (do NOT re-decide what 271c already chose)\n- **The renderer module and its pandoc delivery are owned by 271c.** Story\n  `[rebar:271c-e88f-3220-4c9b]` ships the pure renderer at\n  `adapters/jira_family/wiki_render.py` and delivers pandoc via the\n  **`pypandoc-binary` wheel** (the pandoc binary is BUNDLED IN THE WHEEL and pinned\n  in `pyproject.toml`), specifically so the DC path is self-contained in CI and\n  prod with no system pandoc install. This story hardens THAT module and pins THAT\n  wheel; it does **not** introduce a separate system-level pandoc or an\n  `acli`-style version+sha binary pin (acli is a symlink + `acli jira auth login`\n  system prerequisite, not a pinned pip binary — there is no acli pin pattern to\n  mirror).\n- 271c leaves `WikiTextCodec` fully identity and delivers `wiki_render.py` as a\n  **pure module not referenced by any codec op or live send path in that story**\n  (271c's own words); all live wiring — the codec ops AND the live send sites — is\n  deferred to the cutover story `[rebar:3388-96e8-e14e-4221]` and is **out of scope\n  here**. This story touches only the pure `wiki_render.py` module functions, never\n  the codec or the live send.\n- **The change-gated \"no-op → no pandoc spawn\" property is delivered by the\n  cutover/echo story `[rebar:3388-96e8-e14e-4221]`**, which introduces the\n  change-gated `rich_sha` render gate; that symbol does not yet exist on\n  `origin/main` and is not this story's to add. THIS story owns only the cost,\n  timeout, fallback, batching, and compliance of a render that IS performed.\n\n## Approach\n- **Bound the per-render subprocess cost by batching:** when a changed body IS\n  rendered, batch its structurally-safe units into as few pandoc invocations as\n  correctness allows rather than one spawn per unit. (pandoc 3.9 exposes no\n  `--server`/daemon, confirmed, so a persistent server is out of scope; batching is\n  the available lever.)\n- **Hard timeout + process-group reaping + verbatim fallback, defined coherently\n  WITH batching:** a plain `subprocess.run(timeout=)` / pypandoc timeout only reaps\n  the DIRECT child, so a pandoc jira-reader spinning at ~95.8% CPU (a\n  pipe-holding grandchild) survives and keeps burning the core (repo bug d843,\n  CPython bpo-30154). Therefore the renderer MUST launch pandoc via the shared leaf\n  **`rebar._proc.reap_process_group`** (`_proc.py:26`) — `Popen(start_new_session=\n  True)` + `killpg` SIGTERM→grace→SIGKILL→bounded drain, the exact helper already\n  reused by the ACLI transport and grounding harness — so the whole process group,\n  not just the direct child, is killed on timeout. On timeout or non-zero exit,\n  degrade **every unit in that invocation's batch** to its original Markdown\n  verbatim (plain Markdown is trivially echo-safe), and record a per-unit fallback\n  marker. Because the fallback granularity is the batch, a batch is only ever grown\n  to include units whose combined failure is acceptable to degrade together; a unit\n  already flagged dangerous by 271c's classifier is rendered in its own single-unit\n  invocation so its verbatim fallback cannot pull safe siblings down with it. This\n  removes the batching↔fallback interaction gap: batch boundary == fallback\n  boundary.\n- **Compliance:** pin the `pypandoc-binary` wheel by version in `pyproject.toml`\n  (271c's mechanism), record the wheel-bundled pandoc's GPL license in the project\n  SBOM/notice, and verify the wheel pin in CI.\n\n## Scope\n- `adapters/jira_family/wiki_render.py` — the pure DC renderer module delivered by\n  271c: add the per-invocation timeout with process-group reaping via the shared\n  `rebar._proc.reap_process_group` (`Popen(start_new_session=True)` + `killpg`), the\n  batch-granular verbatim fallback, and unit-batching (dangerous units rendered\n  singly). No codec op and no live-send site is touched here.\n- `pyproject.toml` — pin the `pypandoc-binary` wheel version (extends 271c's pin;\n  no separate system pandoc).\n- CI workflow config — verify the `pypandoc-binary` wheel pin (NOT an acli-style\n  system-binary sha check).\n- SBOM/licensing notice file — record the wheel-bundled pandoc GPL entry.\n- `tests/` — timeout, batch-fallback, and batching tests over the vendored DC\n  corpus fixture.\n\n## Testing\n- Timeout path: a synthetic input that exceeds the configured timeout is killed by\n  process-group reaping (the CPU-spinning pandoc grandchild is gone, not just the\n  direct child) and falls back to verbatim Markdown for every unit in its batch and\n  the render returns; asserted by `test_dc_pandoc_timeout_falls_back` (spawns a\n  child that forks a spinning grandchild; asserts the group PID is reaped).\n- Failure path: a non-zero pandoc exit degrades that batch to plaintext without\n  raising, and the fallback is recorded per unit; `test_dc_pandoc_error_degrades`.\n- Batching: a body of N safe units renders in fewer than N pandoc invocations\n  (spawn-count spy), while a body containing a dangerous unit renders that unit in\n  its own single-unit invocation; `test_dc_render_batches_safe_units`.\n- Batch==fallback boundary: when a batched invocation times out, exactly the units\n  in that batch fall back to verbatim and safe units in OTHER batches still render;\n  `test_dc_batch_fallback_boundary`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Every pandoc invocation runs under a wall-clock timeout with process-group\n  reaping (`rebar._proc.reap_process_group`, so a pipe-holding CPU-spinning\n  grandchild is killed, not orphaned) and, on timeout or non-zero exit, degrades\n  every unit in that invocation's batch to verbatim Markdown without raising; the\n  render returns; verified by `test_dc_pandoc_timeout_falls_back` (asserts the\n  spawned group is reaped) and `test_dc_pandoc_error_degrades`.\n- [ ] A changed body of N structurally-safe units renders in fewer than N pandoc\n  invocations, and a dangerous unit is rendered in its own single-unit invocation\n  so its verbatim fallback cannot degrade safe siblings; verified by\n  `test_dc_render_batches_safe_units` and `test_dc_batch_fallback_boundary` (batch\n  boundary == fallback boundary).\n- [ ] The pandoc binary is provided by the `pypandoc-binary` wheel pinned by\n  version in `pyproject.toml` (271c's mechanism, not a system pandoc), the wheel\n  pin is verified in CI, and the wheel-bundled pandoc GPL license is recorded in\n  the SBOM/notice; verified by the CI wheel-pin check and a notice-file test."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785813625606914001,"uuid":"54c6fd92-71d8-4c93-b432-2b14306a27db"}